 "result": "I've written the Dockerfile and three supporting files, all new. I couldn't build or run anything here, so none of this has been tested.\n\n**Files created:**\n- `Dockerfile`: builds in two stages on Node 22 (`node:22-bookworm-slim`). The first stage installs only the production dependencies with `npm ci --omit=dev`, which leaves out Jest and Puppeteer. It also includes compilers in case `better-sqlite3` has to be built from source. The second stage holds the app and runs `node server/index.js` as the unprivileged `node` user on port 3000.\n- `docker/entrypoint.sh`: the startup script. It stores all data in `/data` (as a volume) and does two things before starting the server:\n  - **First start:** if `/data` has no data files yet, it copies in the demo data that ships in `data/`. It never overwrites existing data.\n  - **`JWT_SECRET` not set:** it generates a random secret once and saves it in `/data/.jwt_secret`, so logins survive restarts.\n- `docker/healthcheck.js`: checks that the login page answers, over HTTP or HTTPS depending on whether certificates are configured.\n- `.dockerignore`: keeps `node_modules`, `.env` files, certificates, tests, logs, the local database and uploads out of the image. It also excludes the copyrighted ISO files that `.gitignore` already blocks.\n\n**Things to know:**\n- **The image runs in production mode.** The server checks this and refuses to start if `JWT_SECRET` is still the placeholder from `.env.docker`. Either put a real secret in that file or leave the variable out so one is generated.\n- **Default demo accounts:** on first start the data includes the demo logins (admin/adminpass and others).\n- **HTTPS:** without certificates or `TRUST_PROXY`, the server logs a warning that it is running over plain HTTP.\n\nTo build and run:\n```\ndocker build -t isms-builder .\ndocker run -d -p 3000:3000 -v isms-data:/data -e JWT_SECRET=\"$(openssl rand -hex 32)\" isms-builder\n```",
